WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
how have we found ourselves in this  position where people are queuing for  hours for fuel  we find ourselves in this position  because of a complete failure of the  government to lead and to plan ahead i  and other shadow cabinet colleagues  wrote to grant chaps back in july  highlighting these issues we got a very  short trip from grant shaps who wrote  back to us in the first week of august  saying in his words that he wouldn't be  using foreign labour to solve this  issue now the government says it wants  to  train up and i'm absolutely in favor of  training up hgv drivers but it hasn't  done that to a sufficient extent  nor has it until recently made a very  small concession on being able to bring  drivers in from abroad so this is a  catastrophic failure of leadership it  looks like we've ended up with petrol  running out the prime minister talking  about bringing the army and this is a  crisis of the government's own making um  hgv drivers being offered a three-month  contract pointless is what we're being  told by industry insiders what would  labour do  uh well we've already said that at this  stage you'd have to be looking at a far  larger number than that and you'd have  to be looking at more attractive and  longer visas and the number you would  need is what you would need to solve the  problem but also i i i asked other  shadow cabinet members asked months ago  about planning for this eventuality it's  this government that has ended up in  this situation through its chronic lack  of planning and sheer incompetence so  what number would you be looking at  well the gap at the moment is 100 000.  now i would much i would like to be in a  situation where we were able to do both  things having been training up drivers  to take up those positions and labor  would have been doing that for months  and months in a way this government  clearly isn't then because it's a crisis  you fill the gap by offering visas but  you have to be confident that the visas  that you are offering will attract  enough drivers so you've got to fill  that gap but the reason the government  is in such a desperate situation is it  hasn't been doing what it promised to do  both about training new hgv drivers but  also planning for the different  scenarios this autumn so what would the  visa look like if labor was in power  what i would be doing is taking advice  as to precisely what the visa would need  to be to attract the numbers the  government has got lots of different  modeling uh in front of it it will have  lots of advice as to  what the likely number of drivers you  will attract for particular conditions  is it has to i'm afraid look a bit more  than the 5000 and the three months that  is before it if we are to listen to what  uh most it seems across industry is  saying which is that's not going to work  you can't criticize the government for  saying three months is not it is not  enough if you if you don't have a figure  yourselves  well it's about choosing  what precisely it is that is going to  attract the number of drivers think that  might be i mean obviously you've got  your finger on the pulse obviously so  what what figure would labour come up  with  would be taking advice across industry  properly okay as to what best figure  what best that is okay well i i suppose  what i'm trying to get to you mr  mountain thomas this morning is okay  labour's not just sitting on their  bottoms thinking okay it's your problem  i'm guessing you're having those  conversations as well to try and be as  constructive as possible for the people  of great britain my question is as far  as labour is concerned having had all  those conversations as well what do you  think would be um something attractive  enough for european drivers to come to  the uk  but but but on the contrary k we haven't  had those conversations we wrote to  grant chaps in july about this asking  about conversations for what we could do  in terms of solving it we actually asked  grant chaps in july to refer this issue  of visas to the migration advisory  committee to give expert advice on this  and we were completely dismissed and  told that he wasn't going to use foreign  labour at all so it's no fault of ours  that we haven't entered into a  discussion and we haven't had access  that expert advice because that is  precisely what we asked to happen in  july we were just dismissed out of hand  and absolutely if grant chaps had come  back with a constructive response i  would have access to that advice from  the migration advisory committee and  would be in a position to start giving  you some more definitive positions it is  to the government shame that they  wouldn't even work with the opposition  when we raise the scale of this crisis  months in advance  government's right though isn't it that  um we don't need uh the army we're  looking at this petrol station in  manchester as we speak to you and there  are no queues anymore there's only a  finite amount of fuel that people can  put in their vehicles  and the problem will resolve itself  by the end of the week  well it's remarkably complacent if the  government is just sat there assuming  what is going to happen in a few days  and i do worry the government isn't  taking responsibility we have a prime  minister on the one hand was saying  don't panic on the other he was saying  that he needed to bring the army in so i  think you have to look at the mixed  messaging from the government and as  always with this government when it hits  a crisis it's always apparently somebody  else's fault rather than their failure  to grip it and to plan for different  scenarios in advance problems uh not in  manchester this morning as we can see  certainly at that fuel station but there  are in many other parts of the country  should emergency and essential workers  be able to jump the queue  i think we do need to look at that  absolutely but again  you're putting that question to me k and  then on the other the government is  saying the problem is going to resolve  itself so yet again you get into mixed  messaging but what an appalling position  the government finds itself in we are  here in 2021 we are rationing petrol  we have a government that is threatening  to bring the army in and we have a  supply crisis  all of which the government has been  warned about the government should have  listened about and through thorough  incompetence it now finds itself in this  quite awful position uh this september  okay you have those pesky ear pieces  they're really irritating aren't they  hopefully you can still um hear me as  i'm chatting to you um nick so are you  saying that that the um emergency  workers should be able to jump the queue  a bit like two decades ago when when  labour had a similar issue getting  petrol to the pumps and there were  special passes that were issued for  emergency workers to be able to get fuel  ahead of others  well in crisis situations you've clearly  got to prioritize key workers it isn't  as if you know that that's a contested  definition i mean the government has  used that key worker definition  throughout the pandemic i'm thinking for  example in the lockdowns when you know  key workers were still allowed to go out  to work so the government has all that  on the books already as to who is a key  worker and of course if you're  prioritizing keeping the country running  that's what you have to do but goodness  me  prioritizing keeping the country running  with a government that is rationing  petrol this is quite an appalling  situation that this government has now  reached and there's no one to blame for  it apart from them and their  incompetence you're going to tell a  conference that labour should be um  tough on crime what should what would  labour do about those protesters on the  m25  in terms of the protesters on the m25 of  course i am very passionate about  tackling climate change however i do not  believe that unlawful protest is the way  to go about it and i've been very clear  that i support action that the police  the operational decisions police are  taking on the on the front line i also  support the use of injunctions because  the problem is that when police do  arrest people for for breaking the law  which they are breaking the law they can  only stay in custody for a limited  period and they can come back so i think  the use of injunctions is also very  important but the underlying issue of  climate change absolutely i and labour  are very passionate about tackling  um andy mcdonald my goodness me um  former uh colleague of yours in the  shadow cabinet what's all that about  well look uh people have resigned on  points of principle before and i always  respect people who take that decision  andy is a friend of mine who will  continue to be a friend of mine  and i have great respect for the work  he's done on the employment rights paper  where we will end appalling practices  like fire and rehire we will give people  employment rights from day one but i  must say in terms of the motion that's  before conference today the leadership  is very happy for delegates to back it  we are very committed to a minimum wage  of at least 10 pounds an hour but it is  the responsible thing as we get closer  to the election to look at rate of  inflation rate of wages the wider  economic situation for the precise  figure that we will put before the  country at the next general election  but we are fighting for working people  now whether it is in terms of a minimum  wage of at least 10 pound which by the  way would be a 12 percent increase in  wages for many people whether it's in  terms of cancelling the universal credit  cut whether it's speaking against the  government's plans to hike national  insurance which are frankly attacks on  working people as well because i wish  andy well on the back benches  yeah but why did he have to do it at  party conference we broke the news to  rachel rave tamara cohen did that um  yesterday she looked visibly shocked at  the discussion at the news i should say  i mean why wash your dirty linen in  public labor seems to do this year after  year you've got open goals left right  and center and you and you're washing  your dirty laundry in public  well look only only andy can answer the  question as to why he chose on that  particular day to resign it's always a  difficult decision to make but what i'm  seeing at this conference is rule  changes from kiyostama to ensure that  the party will now be able to look  outwards people said that those rule  changes wouldn't get through they told  here to back off but he showed strong  leadership and got them through we're  now in a situation where rachel reeve  sat out her store yesterday i'll be  doing that today on labour's priority on  neighborhood policing making clear that  if there is trouble on your street labor  will make sure that somebody is there  and setting out my plans to put police  hubs neighborhood prevention teams to  tackle anti-social behavior across the  country and tomorrow you will hear from  kiyostama with his vision for the  country as well

